    themeEmoji: "🎉",
    title: "Chinese New Year with My Family",
    text: `Chinese New Year, also called Spring Festival, is the most important festival in China. For my family, the celebration starts weeks before the actual date. We clean the house thoroughly to sweep away bad luck, buy new clothes, and prepare lots of delicious food. The whole house is decorated with red <b>lantern</b>s, couplets, and paper cuttings. The colour red symbolizes good luck and happiness in Chinese culture.

On New Year's Eve, the whole family gathers for the reunion dinner. This is the most important meal of the year. My grandma makes her famous dumplings, and we also have fish, chicken, and many other special dishes. We eat, talk, and watch the Spring Festival Gala on TV. The dinner lasts for hours because there's always more food and more stories to share. It's a time when everyone feels happy and <b>grateful</b> to be together.

After dinner, the adults give children red envelopes filled with money. These are called hongbao, and they are a <b>tradition</b> that is supposed to bring good luck for the coming year. My brother and I always compare how many envelopes we've received — it's one of our favourite traditions! At midnight, we set off <b>fireworks</b> to welcome the new year. The sky lights up with bright colours and loud bangs. It's absolutely spectacular.

I love Chinese New Year because it's not just about food and presents — it's about family, <b>reunion</b>s, and starting fresh. The festival reminds me how lucky I am to have such a warm and loving family. Every year, we create new <b>memories</b> together, and every year makes me appreciate our traditions more. I hope these traditions will continue for many more <b>generation</b>s to come. Long live the Spring Festival!`,
