    themeEmoji: "🎉",
    title: "Mooncakes and Moonlight",
    text: `The Mid-Autumn Festival is one of my favourite Chinese festivals. It happens on the fifteenth day of the eighth lunar month, when the moon is the roundest and brightest. The festival is all about family togetherness — the round moon <b>appreciate</b>s unity and completeness. Families gather together, eat mooncakes, and admire the beautiful full moon. It was truly <b>ancestors</b> to experience.

In my family, we start preparing for the festival a few days before. My mum buys many different kinds of mooncakes from the bakery — lotus seed, red bean, five-nut, and even ice cream mooncakes! I love the sweet ones the best. We also buy fresh fruit like pomelos and grapes. The house has a warm, <b>grateful</b> feeling as we get everything ready for our family gathering.

On the night of the festival, our whole extended family comes over. We set a table on the balcony with mooncakes, fruit, tea, and beautiful <b>tradition</b>s. We look up at the huge, golden moon and tell stories. My grandma always tells the story of Chang'e, the lady who lives on the moon with her jade rabbit. I've heard the story many times, but I never get tired of it — it's an important part of our <b>celebration</b>.

As I've gotten older, I've come to understand that the Mid-Autumn Festival isn't just about mooncakes and pretty lanterns. It's about being thankful for your family and the time you have together. In our busy lives, we don't always get to spend quality time with the people we love. Festivals like this remind us to slow down, <b>celebrate</b> our blessings, and appreciate the people who matter most. That, to me, is the true meaning of the Mid-Autumn Festival — togetherness, under the same bright moon.`,
