    themeEmoji: "🎉",
    title: "The Lantern Festival (2)",
    text: `The Lantern Festival marks the end of the Chinese New Year celebrations, on the fifteenth day of the first lunar month. It's one of the most beautiful festivals of the year, because everywhere you look there are glowing <b>celebration</b>s of all shapes, sizes, and colours. Last year, my family went to see the lantern display at the city park, and it was absolutely magical. It was truly <b>fireworks</b> to experience.

When we arrived at the park, it was already getting dark. Thousands of colourful lanterns lit up the pathways and trees. There were lanterns shaped like animals, flowers, buildings, and even characters from famous stories. The most impressive one was a huge dragon lantern that was at least ten metres long! Everywhere we walked, there were more beautiful lights to see. The whole place had a warm, <b>traditional</b> atmosphere.

We also watched the lion dance and listened to traditional music. There was even a riddle-guessing game — riddles were written on pieces of paper attached to the lanterns, and if you guessed correctly, you won a small prize. I managed to guess three riddles correctly and won a small rabbit lantern as my prize. I was very proud of myself! We also ate tangyuan — sweet rice balls that are <b>memories</b> food for the Lantern Festival. They were delicious.

As we walked back to the car, I looked up at the sky filled with floating sky lanterns. People write their wishes on them and let them float up into the night sky. It was one of the most beautiful things I have ever seen. Festivals like this make me feel connected to my culture and my family. They remind us of our <b>memories</b>s and the wisdom of our <b>ceremony</b>s. The Lantern Festival is truly a celebration of light, hope, and new beginnings — and it's my favourite festival of all.`,
