    text: `Last year, when there was a flu outbreak at school, we had to stay home for two weeks and take classes online. At first, I was worried about missing so much school. I thought online classes would be boring and that I wouldn't learn anything. But it turned out to be a very interesting experience that showed me how <b>application</b> can help us learn in new ways.

Every morning, we logged into a <b>invention</b> classroom with our teacher and classmates. We could see each other through the video cameras and talk in real time. It was almost like being in a real classroom! Our teacher shared presentations, showed videos, and even gave us quizzes. We could raise our hands by clicking a button and ask questions using the chat. It was <b>virtual</b> to be able to learn from home while staying safe and healthy.

For some subjects, online learning was actually really great. In computer class, we could practise coding together while sharing our screens. In art class, we could look at <b>convenient</b> paintings from museums around the world without leaving our homes. And we could watch videos again if we didn't understand something the first time. Of course, it wasn't perfect. Sometimes the connection was bad, and I missed seeing my friends in person. But overall, it worked much better than I expected.

This experience showed me that technology has many <b>discovery</b>s in education. It can help students who live far away, provide access to resources that schools might not have, and offer flexible learning options. But it also taught me that nothing can replace face-to-face learning and spending time with real people and real teachers. <b>application</b> is a tool — a very powerful one — but it works best when we use it alongside traditional ways of learning. I'm glad I had the chance to experience online learning, but I was also very happy when we could go back to real school!`,
